RED on 6f3dc7c (3 failures, evidence/S7_red.txt), two verified defects:

(1) clear_engagement_target_hosts(None) reset the contextvars but NEVER
unregistered the process-wide _ACTIVE_ENGAGEMENTS entry (only the
structured-token path unregistered). After any force-clear — used by
many test fixtures and any fail-closed teardown —
_any_engagement_active() stayed True forever, and
is_engagement_origin_allowed took the B6 'context was LOST' branch,
DENYING every later request in the process: fail-closed masquerading
as security. The full suite surfaces this as ~15 order-dependent
failures.

(2) The token path unregistered with the key of token.previous while
registration used the key of the NEW context — identical today only
because both derive to 'default'; real authority contexts carry
scope_fingerprint (scope/authority.py ~493, ~563), making the leak
GUARANTEED on every real engagement.

Fix: the force path empties the registry completely under
_ENGAGEMENT_LOCK (a force teardown leaves nothing alive — the token
paths unregister only their own entries); EngagementToken carries
registry_key (default 'default') set from _registry_key_of(context) at
both constructor sites, and teardown unregisters with EXACTLY that key
(_unregister_registry_key).

Contract suite (4, evidence/S7_green.txt): force-clear empties the
registry; a context-less caller (fresh contextvars context) is DENIED
during the campaign (B6 preserved) then ALLOWED again after the
engagement truly ends; force-clear empties every key (multi-key
scenario); the token path unregisters exactly its own entry (inner
fingerprint survives the outer token's teardown). Autouse registry
hygiene fixture after every test — the pollution lesson.

Wings: B6 (both files) + 34 + 41 (all three) = 63/63 after aligning
the umdr34 ceiling-parity default assertion to the B6(d) contract
(mission rule 9: rate_limit_enabled default flipped False->True — the
ONE authorized default change; the engagement ceiling itself is
unchanged and its independence is still proven by explicit toggles).
